    "two_skillets": {
      "type": "custom-liquid",
      "name": "t:sections.custom-liquid.presets.name",
      "settings": {
        "section_id": "two-skillets",
        "liquid": "\n<style>\n  .sea-two { --ink:#171717; --muted:#5c5c5c; --line:#e5e2dc; --wash:#faf8f4; --accent:#D45A3C;\n    color: var(--ink); }\n  .sea-two__eyebrow { font-size:11px; letter-spacing:.14em; text-transform:uppercase;\n    color:var(--accent); font-weight:600; margin:0 0 8px; }\n  .sea-two__heading { font-size:clamp(22px,3.2vw,31px); line-height:1.22; margin:0 0 14px; font-weight:600; }\n  .sea-two__lede { font-size:17px; line-height:1.65; color:var(--muted); margin:0 0 26px; max-width:62ch; }\n  .sea-two__grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:16px; }\n  .sea-two__card { border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:16px; background:var(--wash); padding:20px 22px; }\n  .sea-two__card--fix { border-color:var(--accent); }\n  .sea-two__tag { display:inline-block; font-size:10px; letter-spacing:.08em; font-weight:700;\n    text-transform:uppercase; padding:3px 9px; border-radius:999px; margin:0 0 9px;\n    background:var(--ink); color:#fff; }\n  .sea-two__card--fix .sea-two__tag { background:var(--accent); }\n  .sea-two__name { font-size:17px; font-weight:600; margin:0 0 7px; line-height:1.3; }\n  .sea-two__note { font-size:14.5px; line-height:1.6; color:var(--muted); margin:0; }\n  .sea-two__foot { font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6; color:var(--muted);\n    margin:20px 0 0; padding-top:14px; border-top:1px solid var(--line); max-width:70ch; }\n  @media (max-width:749px) { .sea-two__grid { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }\n</style>\n\n<div class=\"sea-two\">\n  <p class=\"sea-two__eyebrow\">About that &ldquo;one pan&rdquo;</p>\n  <h2 class=\"sea-two__heading\">The fish gets its own skillet, and that is the whole trick.</h2>\n  <p class=\"sea-two__lede\">\n    Everything else genuinely is one pan. The salmon and branzino are the exception, and it is\n    worth the second piece of washing up: skin crisps in dry heat and goes soft in liquid, so\n    laying raw fillets into the tomato base means poaching them and losing the crackle entirely.\n  </p>\n\n  <div class=\"sea-two__grid\">\n    <div class=\"sea-two__card\">\n      <span class=\"sea-two__tag\">What goes wrong</span>\n      <p class=\"sea-two__name\">Fish cooked in the base</p>\n      <p class=\"sea-two__note\">\n        The skin steams instead of searing and turns slack and grey. The fillet also overshoots\n        easily, because a simmering pan holds far more heat against it than a dry skillet does.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"sea-two__card sea-two__card--fix\">\n      <span class=\"sea-two__tag\">What to do</span>\n      <p class=\"sea-two__name\">Crisp separately, finish off the heat</p>\n      <p class=\"sea-two__note\">\n        Sear skin-down in a hot dry pan, then set the fillets into the base and let residual heat\n        carry them the rest of the way. Break them into large pieces at the last moment so the\n        crisp skin sits proud of the broth rather than sinking into it.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p class=\"sea-two__foot\">\n    Same reasoning behind the order of the seafood: shrimp poach in the base because that is\n    exactly what suits them, and the roe never sees heat at all &mdash; it goes on at the table,\n    where it stays as distinct beads instead of collapsing.\n  </p>\n</div>",
        "padding_top": 12,
        "padding_bottom": 44,
        "divider": false,
        "narrow": false,
        "rounded": false,
        "color_text": "",
        "color_background": "",
        "gradient_background": "",
        "color_button_text": "",
        "color_button_background": "",
        "color_button_gradient": ""
      }
    },
