          }
        },
        "f_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why four kinds instead of twice as much of one",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "f_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Each one is doing a different job. King salmon is fatty enough to hold up to a tomato base that would flatten a leaner fish. Branzino is delicate and flakes into the broth. Shrimp bring the snap. The roe is texture and salinity, added at the table and never cooked.</p><p>Doubling any single one gives you a good dish. Using all four is what makes it feel like a occasion, and it is the reason the variety box exists.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
