    "faq_skillet": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_onepan": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "It says one pan but I counted two. Which is it?",
            "text": "<p>The base &mdash; aromatics, tomatoes, beans, kale and the shrimp &mdash; is genuinely one skillet, and that is the pan it is served from. The salmon and branzino get a second pan for a few minutes so the skin crisps. Cook them in the base and you will have poached fish with soft skin, which is a perfectly nice dinner but not this one.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_doneness": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the fish is done?",
            "text": "<p>Pull it while the centre is still slightly translucent and let the residual heat in the base finish it. Salmon carries on cooking for several minutes after it leaves the pan, so fish that looks perfect in the skillet is usually overcooked by the time it reaches the table.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_skin": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "My skin never crisps. What am I doing wrong?",
            "text": "<p>Moisture or movement, nearly always. Pat the skin drier than feels reasonable, salt it, and get the pan properly hot before the fish goes in. Then leave it alone &mdash; press it flat for the first few seconds and do not touch it again until the skin releases on its own.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_ahead": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make it ahead?",
            "text": "<p>Make the base up to two days ahead and refrigerate it; it gets better. Reheat gently, wilt the kale in, then cook the shrimp and crisp the fish fresh. Neither reheats well.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_tomatoes": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if my tomatoes are not ripe?",
            "text": "<p>Use about &frac34; cup of passata instead of juicing them, and finish with extra lemon zest to put back the brightness. Out-of-season tomatoes give you water and not much else, and the juice is doing real work here.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of cold water for 20&ndash;30 minutes. Not at room temperature and not in warm water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_onepan",
